Lory

Lory reimplements, in Crow, behaviour that the libraries below publish. No
source of theirs is included. Their rules and formulas were read from their
documentation and written anew, and their numbers were measured by running
pinned versions of them: each area's reference script, under tests/, runs
the library and writes what Lory is held to.

- Floating UI (@floating-ui/core 1.7.5), MIT, Copyright (c) 2021-present
  Floating UI contributors. gui's layers are placed against their controls
  as it places them (offset, shift with limitShift, flip), held to its
  answers in 1,200 cases.

- culori 4.0.2, MIT, Copyright (c) Dan Cristea. lory.colors' conversions
  between colour spaces (linear sRGB, OKLab, OKLCH, HSL, HSV) and its gamut
  fitting are held to its numbers.

- APCA, the Accessible Perceptual Contrast Algorithm, by Andrew Somers
  (Myndex Research). lory.colors' `apca` is Lory's own implementation of
  the formula Myndex published (its 0.0.98G-4g constants), held to the
  answers of apca-w3 0.1.9. It includes none of apca-w3's code; that
  package's own licence covers that package.

- Radix Colors 3.0.0 and Radix Themes 3.2.1, MIT, Copyright (c) 2022
  WorkOS. lory.colors' twelve-step scales follow the uses Radix gives each
  step, and its themes answer the tokens Radix Themes does. Lory's scales
  are generated by Lory and measured against Radix's, never copied.

- Vuetify 3.7.6, MIT, Copyright (c) 2016-present Vuetify, LLC. The names of
  its colour palette and their shades, and the text it puts on a colour
  (getForeground), held to its answers.

- Semantic UI 2.4.1, MIT, Copyright (c) 2015 Semantic Org. Its named
  colours' hover, focus, pressed and active shades, as its LESS computes
  them, held to LESS 4.2.0's answers (Apache-2.0) on Lory's own colours.
  lory.icons turns, flips and spins an icon as its icon.less does.

- Radix Icons, MIT, Copyright (c) 2022 WorkOS. lory.icons answers to the
  names Radix Icons gives its 318 icons (all but the brand marks), so a
  program finds an icon by the name it knows. Every drawing is Lory's own,
  made from the name alone; none of Radix's artwork is drawn or traced.

- anime.js 4.5.0, MIT, Copyright (c) 2025 Julian Garnier; Motion 14.7.1
  (motion, motion-dom 14.7.1, motion-utils 14.7.0), MIT, Copyright (c)
  2024 Motion B.V., framer-motion Copyright (c) 2018 Framer B.V.; and AOS
  2.3.4, MIT, Copyright (c) 2015 Michał Sajnóg. lory.motion's easings,
  springs, inertia, staggers, timelines, timers, keyframes and scroll
  progress follow their formulas, read and written anew, and are held to
  their answers: 9,840 numbers, 96.8% of them bit for bit and the rest
  within 2 parts in 2^52 (tests/motion/reference.co).

- lory.space draws three's materials, lights, fog, colour management,
  clipping, instancing and basic shadow maps as three 0.187.0's shaders
  compute them, read and written anew; the standard material's DFG table
  is not copied (an analytic fit stands in). lory.controls and geometry's
  svg_paths, marched and arrow follow three's FlyControls,
  TrackballControls, DragControls, SVGLoader's path reading, MarchingCubes
  and ArrowHelper, written anew.

- three.js 0.187.0, MIT, Copyright 2010-2026 three.js authors, and earcut
  3.0.2 within it, ISC, Copyright (c) Mapbox. lory.geometry lays out its
  shapes, curves, paths, extrusions, polyhedra, edges and wireframes as
  three's BufferGeometry classes do, and cuts shapes into triangles as
  earcut does, held to three's numbers bit for bit in 108 cases. The order
  of the regular polyhedra's faces is three's, kept so that every index is
  three's; Lory works out their corners itself.

